#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23bb654c-f3f2-51f2-a304-986abb4ccd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1+0.9e^{-30t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23bb654c-f3f2-51f2-a304-986abb4ccd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1+0.9e^{-30t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334084} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31f1f3e-89e3-5572-a496-777194375ee5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "436b4239-0995-5c04-8091-48d946d7aba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336198" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/b437d93e812512302f48421f23fb048f2cfb9569f7eb3577449a24dfebdfec50.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3=30I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+30I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+30{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-30t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+30\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-30t}+\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-30(0)}+\\frac{1}{10} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{9}{10}{e}^{-30t}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(9{e}^{-30t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{9}{10}{e}^{-30t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{10}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f4d16e-1657-5c2d-a9f3-a322641cd45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25+1.75e^{-12t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334087} {:answer/id #uuid "194a792f-735a-52da-884b-2df8e445c737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f4d16e-1657-5c2d-a9f3-a322641cd45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25+1.75e^{-12t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334087} :answer-field/id #uuid "09818c14-0e5f-5aea-8749-67bc1fb7f85d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bcccd92-c7fd-5f0b-b5fb-a1274956455c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336650" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/9f527104795b8d264b0d526ccd6c2cd5c2790e4833fa24b169703170715efe12.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current $I(t)$ (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, given that the current when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=24I+2\\cdot \\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+12\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-12(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-12t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-12t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-12t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af02e35-62c1-5658-8621-d70422bd3c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af02e35-62c1-5658-8621-d70422bd3c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334091} :answer-field/id #uuid "2afd5fae-874d-5bb1-b931-d83561f37742" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fa2462a-cb5a-5c3c-9e01-8a0d6984047b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336654" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.05$ seconds given that the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.05$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=18I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-6(0)}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.05)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6(0.05)}+1)≈0.87\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b24b4560-eb8d-55c3-b62f-6116a6c43e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334094} {:answer/id #uuid "6f54e5ce-10aa-52b5-87ce-90a6c8245ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334095} {:answer/id #uuid "0e52bbe1-86a1-5619-98b7-299cd56bd0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334096} {:answer/id #uuid "f5264547-23a6-56f6-8034-86ac1dd60fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334097} {:answer/id #uuid "67223721-00d8-5047-8194-0127c866041f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5264547-23a6-56f6-8034-86ac1dd60fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334097} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a2b00d8-19c2-5dfc-81f3-86dbb69eca88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b41f053-f95e-5fae-8bb1-5f9cea32f246" :question/math-academy-id "q-336741" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/00d6676b631bd49c0c9b8a82f8dd05de3be6842f18b0c1c6b70f208e1d290d5a.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(12\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 12\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},$ $\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},\\,\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{30}{169}+\\frac{72}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8472e41-a498-5b92-a240-9127ba8b5e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8472e41-a498-5b92-a240-9127ba8b5e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334101} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be48192-2463-56bc-8ceb-400f12d06c43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7b7382d-e971-553e-8ef1-a84dd2bf4e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-336818" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056554/assets/1a2cc9b62f3eeed4f2c9559c7f8cc5646872bc43b8076bea92bae50c3c6b29a9.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=8I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=8I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +8\\beta =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}}≈1.34\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
